# Unity ↔ Cursor bridge (token-efficient)

When inspecting or changing the live Unity editor for PolyPets:

1. **Use the bridge** under `.cursor/unity/` instead of reading `*.unity` YAML, taking screenshots, or slurping `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Unity\Editor\Editor.log`.
2. Run helpers via PowerShell from the repo root:
   - `powershell -File .cursor/unity/Invoke-Unity.ps1 ping`
   - `powershell -File .cursor/unity/Invoke-Unity.ps1 hierarchy -Depth 2 -Limit 40`
   - `powershell -File .cursor/unity/Invoke-Unity.ps1 find -Name Care -Type CareHudController`
   - `powershell -File .cursor/unity/Invoke-Unity.ps1 get-component -Name CareHud -Type CareHudController`
   - `powershell -File .cursor/unity/Invoke-Unity.ps1 log -Limit 25 -Filter Null`
   - `powershell -File .cursor/unity/Invoke-Unity.ps1 exec -Menu "PolyPets/UI/Apply Cozy Companion HUD"`
 - `powershell -File .cursor/unity/Invoke-Unity.ps1 screenshot -View game` (or `scene` / `both`)
 - `powershell -File .cursor/unity/Invoke-Unity.ps1 click -X 240 -Y 650 -Filter topleft` (Play Mode)
3. Requires **Unity Editor open** on this project (`CursorUnityBridge` auto-starts). If `status.json` is missing, tell the user to open Unity.
4. Outputs are capped JSON. Screenshots go to `.cursor/unity/captures/` — Read the PNG path when you need visuals; do not dump full scene YAML / Editor.log.
5. Prefer `get-component` to verify serialized refs (e.g. CareHudController buttons) over opening the scene asset.
